---
import { Code as Shiki } from 'astro:components';
import Prose from '../layouts/Prose.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import { PRIMER } from '../lib/lwon/index.ts';
import { accuracy, basicPerQuestion, change, medianSaving, primerTokens, questionCount, sparse } from '../lib/results.ts';
import { url } from '../lib/url.ts';

const typical = medianSaving;
const breakEven = Math.round(primerTokens / typical / 50) * 50;
const main = accuracy.filter((a) => !a.label.includes('effort'));
const total = main.reduce((s, a) => s + a.cells.lwon.total, 0);
const correct = (f: 'json' | 'lwon') => main.reduce((s, a) => s + a.cells[f].correct, 0);
const sonnet = basicPerQuestion.find((r) => r.label.includes('Sonnet'))!;
const sparseSonnet = sparse.perQuestion.find((r) => r.label.includes('Sonnet'))!;
const themes = { light: 'github-light-default', dark: 'github-dark-default' } as const;

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'where', text: 'Where the savings come from' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'before', text: 'Convert before the model reads it' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'primer', text: 'Tell the model about the format' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'writing', text: 'Reading, not writing' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'mcp', text: 'MCP servers' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'accuracy', text: 'Accuracy' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'cost', text: 'When it pays off' },
];
---
<Prose title="For AI" description="How to use LWON to cut the tokens that tool results, API responses and retrieved records cost in LLM prompts." headings={headings}>
  <h1>LWON for AI</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Models read structured data all day: tool results, API responses, database rows, retrieved
    documents. LWON lets them read the same data for fewer tokens, which means lower cost, lower
    latency and more room in the context window.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where">Where the savings come from</h2>
  <p>
    In a JSON array of 100 records, every key is written 100 times, wrapped in quotes and followed by a
    colon. LWON writes each key once in a header and lists only the values after it. The more records
    and the deeper the nesting, the bigger the gap. See the <a href={url('/benchmarks')}>benchmarks</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="before">Convert before the model reads it</h2>
  <p>
    The tokens are saved only if the model never sees the JSON. Encode in your own code, where the data
    arrives: the tool handler, the API client, the retrieval step.
  </p>
  <Shiki lang="ts" themes={themes} code={`import { encode, PRIMER } from '@lwon/core';

const rows = await db.query('select id, name, plan, mrr from accounts limit 200');

const response = await client.messages.create({
  model: 'claude-sonnet-5-5',
  system: PRIMER,                     // explain the format once
  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: \`Which accounts churned?\\n\\n\${encode(rows)}\` }],
});`} />
  <p>
    Giving the model an <code>encode</code> tool does <strong>not</strong> help: to call it, the model
    would first have to read the JSON, so the tokens are already spent.
  </p>

  <h2 id="primer">Tell the model about the format</h2>
  <p>
    Models do not know LWON yet, so send this primer once per conversation, ideally in the system prompt
    where prompt caching makes it nearly free after the first call. It is about {primerTokens} Claude tokens and
    is exported by the library as <code>PRIMER</code>.
  </p>
  <Code code={PRIMER} lang="text" label="Primer" />
  <p>
    At the typical saving in our benchmarks ({Math.round(typical * 100)}%), the primer pays for itself once
    the data would take about {breakEven} tokens as minified JSON. Below that, send JSON. With a cached
    system prompt the threshold is much lower.
  </p>

  <h2 id="writing">Reading, not writing</h2>
  <p>
    Start by using LWON for data the model <em>reads</em>. Asking a model to <em>write</em> LWON saves
    output tokens, but positional formats are easy to get subtly wrong. If you do, always check the
    output with <code>decode</code> and retry on an error, which includes the line and column.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mcp">MCP servers</h2>
  <p>
    Many MCP servers return large JSON tool results. <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@lwon/mcp-proxy"><code>@lwon/mcp-proxy</code></a>
    sits between your client and any stdio MCP server and turns those results into LWON before the model
    sees them. The server needs no changes: put <code>npx -y @lwon/mcp-proxy --</code> in front of the
    command that starts it.
  </p>
  <Shiki lang="bash" themes={themes} code={`claude mcp add github -- npx -y @lwon/mcp-proxy -- npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-github`} />
  <p>In Claude Desktop's <code>claude_desktop_config.json</code>, or a project's <code>.mcp.json</code>:</p>
  <Shiki lang="json" themes={themes} code={`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "github": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@lwon/mcp-proxy", "--", "npx", "-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"],
      "env": { "GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN": "..." }
    }
  }
}`} />
  <p>
    The proxy converts a <code>tools/call</code> result only when it is JSON, at least 2,000 characters
    long and at least 10% shorter as LWON; everything else passes through unchanged. It adds the primer
    to the server's instructions, so the model reads it once per session. It runs on your machine as a
    child process of your client, so no data goes anywhere else. Options, such as which tools to convert,
    are in the <a href="https://github.com/ahmetzencirli/lwon/tree/main/packages/mcp-proxy#options">README</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="accuracy">Accuracy</h2>
  <p>
    Fewer tokens only help if the model still understands the data. Claude Haiku, Sonnet and Opus 5.5
    each answered {questionCount} questions about the benchmark data in JSON, TOON and LWON. With LWON
    they got <strong>{correct('lwon')} of {total}</strong> right, the same as with JSON ({correct('json')} of {total}).
    On a harder set of 150 sparse log events, LWON stayed close to JSON; the only misses, in every
    format, were counts over many rows that were off by one to three, or a sum.
    <a href={url('/benchmarks#sparse')}>Full results</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cost">When it pays off</h2>
  <p>
    Fewer input tokens is not the whole bill: models also think about the data, and output tokens cost
    several times more than input. Measured on Claude Sonnet 5.5, the full cost of a question compared
    with JSON was:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Regular data, sent once: {change(sonnet.cells.lwon.once, sonnet.cells.json.once)}.</strong> The main case LWON is for: a tool result or API response read once.</li>
    <li>Regular data, cached and reused for many questions: {change(sonnet.cells.lwon.cached, sonnet.cells.json.cached)}.</li>
    <li>Sparse data with counting, sent once: {change(sparseSonnet.cells.lwon.once, sparseSonnet.cells.json.once)}; cached: {change(sparseSonnet.cells.lwon.cached, sparseSonnet.cells.json.cached)}.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The first version of LWON wrote every key by position. On sparse data that made models count their
    way through rows full of <code>~</code>, and a question cost up to three times as much as with JSON.
    LWON now writes keys most rows lack by name, which brought the cost close to JSON's even there.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use LWON for large payloads the model reads. Keep JSON for payloads of a few hundred tokens, where the
    primer costs more than LWON saves, and check your own prompts if they need exact counts over many
    sparse records, where JSON was marginally more accurate in our tests.
  </p>
</Prose>

<style>
  .lead { margin-bottom: 1.5em; }
</style>
